/Type /StructElem
/S /H5
/T (Paragraph : ___\(1\)___. An argument can be defined as a complex symbolic structure where some parts, known as the premises, offer support to another part, the conclusion. Alternatively, an argument can be viewed as a complex speech act consisting of one or more acts of premising \(which assert propositions in favor of the conclusion\), an act of concluding, and a stated or implicit marker \("hence", "therefore"\) that indicates that the conclusion follows from the premises.___\(2\)___. The relation of support between premises and conclusion can be cashed out in different ways: the premises may guarantee the truth of the conclusion, or make its truth more probable; the premises may imply the conclusion; the premises may make the conclusion more acceptable \(or assertible\).___\(3\)___. But depending on one's explanatory goals, there is also much to be gained from considering arguments as they in fact occur in human communicative practices.___\(4\)___.)
/E (Paragraph : ___\(1\)___. An argument can be defined as a complex symbolic structure where some parts, known as the premises, offer support to another part, the conclusion. Alternatively, an argument can be viewed as a complex speech act consisting of one or more acts of premising \(which assert propositions in favor of the conclusion\), an act of concluding, and a stated or implicit marker \("hence", "therefore"\) that indicates that the conclusion follows from the premises.___\(2\)___. The relation of support between premises and conclusion can be cashed out in different ways: the premises may guarantee the truth of the conclusion, or make its truth more probable; the premises may imply the conclusion; the premises may make the conclusion more acceptable \(or assertible\).___\(3\)___. But depending on one's explanatory goals, there is also much to be gained from considering arguments as they in fact occur in human communicative practices.___\(4\)___.)
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [7]
